WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.443
- [Instructor] We are now going

00:00:01.443 --> 00:00:04.490
to talk about valence electrons,

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.320
and non-valence electrons, which
are known as core electrons

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.870
and so one question that you
might have been asking yourself

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:11.980
this whole time that we've been

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.760
looking at electron configurations
is, what is the point?

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.090
And the point of electron
configurations is,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.570
is they can give us insights

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.360
as to how a given atom
or how a given element

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.400
is likely to react with other atoms.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.150
And so just to make that point,

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.790
or make it a little bit clearer,

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.830
let's look at the electron configuration

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.760
of an element that we'll
see a lot of in chemistry,

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.100
of oxygen.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.790
So oxygen's electron
configuration is what?

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.233
Pause this video and see if
you can work through that.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.430
Well, in a neutral oxygen atom,

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.420
you have eight protons
and eight electrons,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.770
so first you're gonna fill the one shell,

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:54.470
then you are going to start
filling the second shell,

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.770
so you're gonna go 2s2,
so I have four right now,

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.060
I have to have four more,

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.443
so then you're going to have 2p4.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.940
And then notice, if I add
up all the electrons here,

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.340
I have exactly eight electrons.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.680
Now if I'm thinking about
how might oxygen react,

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.690
it's interesting to look at
the outer oxygen electrons.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:18.490
The electrons that are
in the outermost shell.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.230
So the outermost shell is being
described right over here,

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.510
this second shell.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.020
So how many electrons are
in the outermost shell?

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.870
You have six electrons here.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.870
So oxygen has six valence,
valence electrons.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.340
And how many core electrons does it have?

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.060
And the core electrons
generally aren't reactive,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.630
or aren't involved as much in reactions?

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:46.630
It has two core, two core electrons.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.650
Now, why is six valence
electrons interesting?

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.830
Well, atoms tend to be more stable

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.960
when they have a filled outer shell,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.940
or in most examples, at
least a filled SNP subshells

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.500
in their outer shell.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.930
And so in this situation, you say, okay,

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.000
oxygen has six valence electrons,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.890
and oftentimes that's drawn
with a Lewis structure,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.700
and it might look something like this,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.820
where oxygen has one, two, three, four,

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.440
five, six valence electrons,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.190
and you might say, hey, it would be nice

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.000
if oxygen somehow were able
to share, or get ahold of,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.460
two more electrons,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.590
because then that outermost shell

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.910
will have a full number
of eight electrons.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.570
The 2s and the 2p would be
filled then, we would have 2p6.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:35.790
And so you'd say, alright,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.060
well maybe they can grab those
electrons from something else

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.940
and that's actually what
oxygen does a lot of,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.760
it grabs electrons from other things.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.320
You can look at something like calcium.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.190
Pause this video, think
about what the electron

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.650
configuration of calcium is,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.960
and then think about how
calcium is likely to react

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.730
given that atoms tend to be more stable

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.930
when they have a full outer shell,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.933
where both their S and P
subshells are completely filled.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.060
Well, calcium's electron configuration,

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.190
I could do it in noble gas
notation or configuration,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.710
it'd have the electron
configuration of argon,

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.540
and one of the reasons why
the noble gases are so stable

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.910
is that they have a completely full shell.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.120
Argon for example has a
completely full first shell,

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.000
second shell, and third shell,
and then to build calcium,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.990
will then have two electrons
in that fourth shell,

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.183
so it is argon and then 4s2.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.550
So how many valence
electrons does calcium have?

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.680
Well, you could see it right over there,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.980
it has two valence electrons.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.460
What about its core electrons?

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.560
Well, a neutral calcium atom
would have 20 electrons,

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:46.870
'cause it has 20 protons,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.780
so it would have 18 core electrons.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.210
Electrons that are less likely to react.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.170
And so you can say, what's the easiest way

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.950
for calcium to get to a full outer shell?

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.110
Well, instead of trying
to gain six electrons,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.430
it might be a lot easier to
just lose these two electrons.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:08.760
It is actually the case that many times,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.880
calcium will lose electrons,
and become ionized,

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.910
will get a positive charge.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.760
So the big picture here is,

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.580
one of the values of
electron configuration

00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:21.960
is to think about which of your electrons

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.970
are most likely to react.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.170
Those are your valence electrons.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.680
In most cases, your valence electrons

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.330
are going to be your outermost electrons.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:32.470
They're going to be the electrons

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.430
in that outermost shell.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.140
Generally speaking, if
you're talking about elements

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.690
that are in the S block or the P block,

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.550
you can think about how many
valence electrons they have

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.460
just based on what column they're in.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.910
This column right over here
has one valence electron.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.610
This column over here has
two valence electrons.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.420
This column out here has
three valence electrons,

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.990
four valence electrons,
five valence electrons,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.300
six valence electrons, and
seven valence electrons.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.760
The noble gases here,
they are very unreactive,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:04.640
so one way to think about it

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.640
is they are very very very stable,

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.313
they have filled their outer shell.

